# Routes a new webview message to the skill that knows the whole chain.
#
# Adding one means touching a MessageType, a handler, a feature map, and the
# webview request site — and the failure when you miss one is a message that is
# sent and never answered, which presents as a slow surface rather than an error.
rule_id=webview-handler
rule_once=1

rule_match() {
    case "$1" in
        Edit|Write) ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
    esac
    [ -n "$3" ] || return 1
    case "$3" in
        */src/types/messages.ts) return 0 ;;
        */src/types/handlers.ts) return 0 ;;
    esac
    # A brand-new handler map is the other end of the same change.
    case "$3" in
        */handlers/*Handlers.ts) [ -e "$3" ] || return 0 ;;
    esac
    return 1
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
You are changing the webview message contract. Invoke the `webview-command-handler`
skill (Skill tool, skill: "webview-command-handler") first.

A message is only wired when FOUR things agree: the MessageType, the handler, the
feature's handler map, and the call site in the webview. Miss one and the message
is sent and never answered — which shows up as a surface that never finishes
loading, not as an error. Nothing typechecks the gap.

Pattern B applies: a handler ANSWERS by returning its result. `sendMessage` is for
progress pushes only.

This hook fires only once per session.
MSG
}
